ng department and provincial hydro inspection offices canada find out what applies your area also your local electrical utility may also have special requirements for electrical service installation bear mind too that say here applies primarily ordinary single family residences multi family dwellings mobile homes commercial establishments etc are sometimes governed different rules also note that contrary popular belief the and some parts canada canada not wholly owned subsidiary the consequently the nec does not apply canada lots things are the same including voltages line frequencies and the laws physics but there are number crucial differences the regulations where can noted them flagging the relevant passages with nec cec remember that the cec and nec are minimal standards often smart beyond their minimal requirements subject what the nec where can get copy the nec model electrical code devised and published the national fire protection association insurance industry group revised every three years the 1993 version has been released you can buy copy decent bookstore calling them directly 800 344 3555 the code exists several versions there the full text which fairly incomprehensible there abridged edition which has only the sections likely apply most houses and there the nec handbook which contains the authorized commentary the code well the full text that the recommended version unfortunately there handbook for the abridged edition and the full handbook expensive plus shipping and handling subject what the cec where can get copy the canadian standards association organization made various government agencies power utilities insurance companies electrical manufacturers and other organizations the csa publishes csa standard c22 which updated every two three years each province adopts with some amendments this standard and publishes province specific code book since each province publishes its own slightly modified standard would somewhat confusing obtain the csa standard itself this faq cec really means the appropriate provincial standard particular this faq derived from the ontario hydro electrical safety code 20th edition 1990 which turn based csa c22 1990 16th edition while differences exist between the provinces attempt has been made avoid specific ontario detail the appropriate provincial code can obtained from electrical inspection offices your provincial power authority ontario ontario hydro the ontario hydro book isn overly fat about and includes mailed updates hear that these standards are somewhat easier read than the equivalent nec publications don bother asking quebec diy wiring banned throughout the province subject can own wiring extra pointers most places homeowners are allowed their own wiring some they not check with your local electrical inspector most places won permit you wiring other homes for money without license nor are you permitted wiring commercial buildings multiple dwellings duplexes are usually considered semi commercial commercial however many jurisdictions will permit you work semi commercial wiring you supervised licensed electrician you can find one willing supervise you your own wiring important point neat and well what you really want aim for better job than electrician will after all your own home and you your family that might get killed you make mistake electrician has time pressures has the skills and knows the tricks the trade fast safe job this faq consciously given few recommendations that are excess code because feel that reasonable and will impress the inspector the inspector will know that you amateur you have earn his trust the best way doing this spend your time doing neat job possible don cut corners exceed specifications otherwise the inspector may get extremely picky and fault you the slightest transgressions don try hide anything from the inspector use the proper tools don use bread knife strip wires twist wires with your fingers the inspector won like and the results won that safe and takes longer and you more likely stick hunk 12ga wire through your hand that way don handle house wire when very cold below 10c 16f thermoplastic house wire particularly older types become very brittle subject what need the way tools first there the obvious hammer drill few screwdrivers both straight and phillips head you lucky enough live canada find source csa approved devices you need robertson square recess screwdrivers and instead phillips for drilling few holes spade bit and electric drill will you doing lot are working with elderly lumber recommend drill right angle drills are wonderful can rented and screw point auger drill bits these bits pull you through they much faster and less fatiguing even year old hardwood timbers screw driver bits are useful for drills expecially you install your electrical boxes using screws drywall screws work well for stripping wire use real wire stripper not knife ordinary wire cutters don buy the mart combo stripper crimper and bottle opener types you should expect pay for good plier type pair will have sized stripping holes and won nick grab the wire should easy strip wire with one model has small hole the blade for forming exact wire loops for screw terminals there are fancier types autostrip cut but they generally aren necessary and pros usually don use them pair diagonal side cutter pliers are useful for clipping ends constricted places don use these for stripping wire you will need linesman pliers for twisting wires for wire nuts you should have pair needle nose pliers for fiddling inside boxes and closing loops but better form wire loops with loop former hole your wire stripper more accurate you using non metallic cable get cable stripper for removing the sheath what some pros they nick the end the sheath grab the ground wire with pair pliers and simply rip the sheath back using the ground wire zipper and cut the sheath off you shouldn try strip the sheath with knife point because too easy slash the insulation the conductors apparently stanley utility knives fitted with linoleum cutters hooked blades can used strip sheath but there still the possibility that you gouge the conductors for any substantial amount work with armored cable well worth your while invest rotary cable splitter hack saws are tricky use without cutting into the wire the insulation three prong outlet testers are quick check for properly wired outlets about multimeters tell you more but are lot more expensive and probably not worth for most people simple voltage sensor which can detect potential through insulated wire not supplying any devices extremely helpful they cost about radio shack you should have voltage detector check that the wires are dead before doing work them neon bulb version are cheap and work well you get more serious audible alarm type good for tracing circuits without helper though been known lock the drill and hit breakers until the scream stops for running wires through existing walls you need fish tape often two tapes are needed though sometimes bent hanger length thin chain will suffice fish tapes can rented electrical tape lots seriously good and competent wiring job will need very little tape the tape useful for wrapping dicy insulation repair work another use wrap around the body outlets and switches cover the termination screws don this but drywall contractors prefer prevent explosions when the drywall knife collides with live outlet that has cover plate subject what listing the stands for underwriters laboratory used insurance industry organization but now independent and non profit tests electrical components and equipment for potential hazards when something listed that means that the has tested the device and meets their requirements for safety fire shock hazard doesn necessarily mean that the device actually does what supposed just that probably won kill you the does not have power law the you are permitted buy and install non listed devices however insurance policies sometimes have clauses them that will limit their liability case claim made response the failure non listed device furthermore many situations the nec will require that wiring component used for specific purpose listed for that purpose indirectly this means that certain parts your wiring must listed before inspector will approve and occupancy permits issued subject what csa approval every electrical device component must certified the canadian standards association before can sold canada implicit this that all wiring must done with csa approved materials they perform testing similar the bit more stringent except that csa approval required law again like the fire was caused non csa approved equipment your insurance company may not have pay the claim canada there branch organization the called ulc canada ulc does not have power law and seems more liason group between the csa and insurance companies subject are there any cheaper easier read books wiring usa the following three books were suggested our readers residential wiring jeff markell craftsman books carlsbad for isbn 934041 practical electrical wiring residential farm and industrial based the national electrical code ansi nfpa herbert richter and creighton schwan mcgraw hill book wiring simplified richter and schwan park publishing try make sure that the book based the latest nec revision which currently 1990 canada knight authors and publishes book called electrical code simplified there appears version published specific each province and very tied into the appropriate provincial code focuses residential wiring and indispensible for canadian diy ers better get this book than the cec unless you lot wiring answer questions the net updated each time the provincial codes are this book available all diy and hardware stores for less than subject inspections how and what why should get wiring inspected most jurisdictions require that you obtain permit and inspections any wiring that done amongst other more mundane bureaucratic reasons like insurance companies not liking have pay claims permit and inspections provides some assurance that you your family your neighbors subsequent owners your home don get killed lose their homes one night due sloppy wiring job most jurisdictions have the power order you vacate your home order you tear out any wiring done without permit california for instance particularly nasty about this fire starts your home and inspected wiring fault insurance companies will often refuse pay the damage claims general the process goes like this you apply your local inspections office building department for permit you should have sketch detailed drawing what you plan doing this good time ask questions any things you not sure you doing major work they may impose special conditions you require loading calculations and ask other questions this point they will tell you which inspections you will need you installing main panel you will need have the panel and service connections inspected before your power utility will provide connection this sometimes done the local power authority rather than the usual inspectors after installing the boxes and wiring but before the insulation walls you will need rough inspection after the walls are and the wiring complete you will need final inspection subject house doesn meet some these rules and regulations have upgrade general there requirement upgrade older dwellings though there are some exceptions smoke detectors some cases however any new work must done according the latest electrical code also you major work you may required upgrade certain existing portions all your system check with your local electrical inspector subject word voltages 110 115 117 120 125 220 240 one thing where things might get bit confusing the different numbers people bandy about for the voltage circuit one person might talk about 110v another 117v another 120v these are all fact exactly the same thing north america the utility companies are required supply split phase 240 volt feed your house this works out two 120v legs additionally since there are resistive voltage drops the house wiring not unreasonable find 120v has dropped 110v 240v has dropped 220v the time the power reaches wall outlet especially the end extension cord long circuit run for number reasons some historical some simple personal orneryness different people choose call them slightly different numbers this faq has chosen consistent with calling them 110v and 220v except when actually saying what the measured voltage will confusing bit just ignore one thing that might make this little more understandable that the nameplates equipment ofen show the lower 110v instead 120v value what this implies that the device designed operate properly when the voltage drops that low 208v not the same 240v 208v the voltage between phases phase circuit that 120v from neutral any hot 480v the voltage between phases phase circuit that 277v from hot neutral keeping with 110v versus 120v strangeness motors intended run 480v three phase are often labelled 440v subject what does electrical service look like there are logically four wires involved with supplying the main panel with power three them will come from the utility pole and fourth bare wire comes from elsewhere the bare wire connected one more long metal bars pounded into the ground wire buried the foundation sometimes the water supply pipe has metal continuous where the main water pipe entering the house watch out for galvanic action conductivity breaks often between copper and iron pipe this the grounding conductor there make sure that the third prong your outlets connected ground this wire normally carries current one the other wires will white black with white yellow stripes sometimes simply black the neutral wire connected the centre tap cec center tap the nec the distribution transformer supplying the power connected the grounding conductor only one place often inside the panel the neutral and ground should not connected anywhere else otherwise weird and dangerous things may happen furthermore there should only one grounding system home some codes require more than one grounding electrode these will connected together connected the neutral common point still one grounding system adding additional grounding electrodes connected other portions the house wiring unsafe and contrary code you add subpanel the ground and neutral are usually brought separate conductors from the main panel and are not connected together the subpanel still only one neutral ground connection however some situations certain categories separate buildings you actually have provide second grounding electrode consult your inspector the other two wires will usually black and are the hot wires they are attached the distribution transformer well the two black wires are 180 degrees out phase with each other this means you connect something both hot wires the voltage will 220 volts you connect something the white and either the two blacks you will get 110v some panels seem only have three wires coming into them this either because the neutral and ground are connected together different point the meter pole and one wire doing dual duty both neutral and ground some rare occasions the service has only one hot wire 110v only service subject what circuit inside the panel connections are made the incoming wires these connections are then used supply power selected portions the home there are three different combinations one hot one neutral and ground 110v circuit two hots neutral and ground 220v circuit two hots neutral and ground 220v circuit neutral and two 110v circuits with common neutral used for most circuits supplying receptacles and lighting within your house usually used for supplying power major appliances such stoves and dryers they often have need for both 220v and 110v for bringing several circuits from the panel box distribution point usually for special 220v motor circuits electric heaters air conditioners note the the nec frequently permits circuit similar used for stoves and dryers namely that there are two hot wires and wire that does dual duty neutral and ground and connected the frame well providing the neutral for 110v purposes three prong plugs instead four only for stoves dryers connected the main panel when connected most sub panels prong plugs and receptacles are required our not humble opinion this crazy but the nfpa claims that this practice was evaluated for the 1992 nec and found safe check your local codes inquire local practice there are restrictions when this permissible usually wired with three conductor wire black for hot white for neutral and bare for grounding and have one hot wire coloured red the other black bare wire for grounding and white wire for neutral you will sometimes see wired with just black white and ground wire since the white hot this case both the nec and cec requires that the white wire permanently marked the ends indicate that live wire usually done with paint nail polish sometimes electrical tape each circuit attached the main wires coming into the panel through circuit breaker fuse there are few locales circuits that look like except that they have two bare ground wires some places require this for hot tubs and the like one ground frame ground the other attaches the motor this may may not alternative gfci protection subject grounding versus grounded versus neutral according the terminology the cec and nec the grounding conductor for the safety ground the green bare wire the word neutral reserved for the white when you have circuit with more than one hot wire since the white wire connected neutral and the grounding conductor inside the panel the proper term grounded conductor however the potential confusion between grounded conductor and grounding conductor can lead potentially lethal mistakes you should never use the bare wire grounded conductor white wire the grounding conductor even though they are connected together the panel but not subpanels subpanels are fed neutral and ground separately from the main panel usually the trade and common usage the word neutral used for grounded conductor this faq uses neutral simply avoid potential confusion recommend that you use neutral too thus the white wire always except some light switch applications neutral not ground subject what does fuse breaker what are the differences fuses and circuit breakers are designed interrupt the power circuit when the current flow exceeds safe levels for example your toaster shorts out fuse breaker should trip protecting the wiring the walls from melting such fuses and breakers are primarily intended protect the wiring csa approval supposedly indicates that the equipment itself won cause fire fuses contain narrow strip metal which designed melt safely when the current exceeds the rated value thereby interrupting the power the circuit fuses trip relatively fast which can sometimes problem with motors which have large startup current surges for motor circuits you can use time delay fuse one brand fusetron which will avoid tripping momentary overloads fusetron looks like spring loaded fuse fuse can only trip once then must replaced breakers are fairly complicated mechanical devices they usually consist one spring loaded contact which latched into position against another contact when the current flow through the device exceeds the rated value bimetallic strip heats and bends bending trips the latch and the spring pulls the contacts apart circuit breakers behave similarly fusetrons that they tend take longer trip moderate overloads than ordinary fuses with high overloads they trip quickly breakers can reset finite number times each time they trip are thrown when the circuit use some arcing takes place which damages the contacts thus breakers should not used place switches unless they are specially listed for the purpose neither fuses nor breakers limit the current per dead short circuit can cause hundreds sometimes even thousands amperes flow for short period time which can often cause severe damage subject breakers can use fuses statistics show that fuse panels have significantly higher risk causing fire than breaker panels this usually due the fuse being loosely screwed the contacts corroding and heating over time the wrong size fuse being installed the proverbial replace the fuse with penny trick since breakers are more permanently installed and have better connection mechanisms the risk fire considerably less fuses are prone explode under extremely high overload when fuse explodes the metallic vapor cloud becomes conducting path result from complete meltdown the electrical panel melted service wiring through fires the electrical distribution transformer and having your house burn down breakers don this many jurisdictions particularly canada longer permit fuse panels new installations the nec does permit new fuse panels some rare circumstances requiring the special inserts key the fuseholder specific size fuses some devices notably certain large air conditioners require fuse protection addition the breaker the panel the fuse there protect the motor windings from overload check the labeling the unit this usually only large permanently installed motors the installation instructions will tell you you need one subject what size wire should use for amp circuit use gauge wire for amp circuit you can use gauge wire most locales for long run though you should use the next larger size wire avoid voltage drops gauge only slightly more expensive than gauge though stiffer and harder work with here quick table for normal situations size for more than 100 foot runs when the cable conduit ganged with other wires place where they can dissipate heat easily gauge amps don list bigger sizes because starts getting very dependent the application and precise wire type subject where these numbers come from there are two considerations voltage drop and heat buildup the smaller the wire the higher the resistance when the resistance higher the wire heats more and there more voltage drop the wiring the former why you need higher temperature insulation and bigger wires for use conduit the latter why you should use larger wire for long runs neither effect very significant over very short distances there are some very specific exceptions where use smaller wire allowed the obvious one the line cord most lamps don try this unless you certain that your use fits one those exceptions you can never wrong using larger wire subject what does mean this used describe the size and quantity conductors cable the first number specifies the gauge the second the number current carrying conductors the wire but remember there usually extra ground wire means gauge two insulated current carrying wires plus bare ground wire usually has black white and bare ground wire sometimes the white red instead for 220v circuits without neutral the latter case the sheath usually red too wire usually has black red white and bare ground wire usually carrying 220v with neutral subject what wirenut marrette marr connector how are they used wire nut cone shaped threaded plastic thingummy that used connect wires together marrette marr connector are trade names you usually use lot them diy wiring essence you strip the end the wires about inch twist them together then twist the wirenut though some wirenuts advertise that you don need twist the wire anyways more mechanically and electrically secure there are many different sizes wire nut you should check that the wire nut you using the correct size for the quantity and sizes wire you connecting together don just gimble the wires together with pair pliers your fingers use pair blunt nose linesman pliers and carefully twist the wires tightly and neatly sometimes good idea trim the resulting end make sure goes the wirenut properly some people wrap the open end the wirenut with electrical tape this probably not good idea the inspector may tear off during inspection usually done because bit bare wire exposed outside the wire nut instead taping the connection should redone subject what gfi gfci gfci ground fault circuit interrupter measures the current current flowing through the hot wire and the neutral wire they differ more than few milliamps the presumption that current leaking ground via some other path this may because short circuit the chassis appliance the ground lead through person any these situations hazardous the gfci trips breaking the circuit gfcis not protect against all kinds electric shocks for example you simultaneously touched the hot and neutral leads circuit and part you was grounded gfci wouldn help all the current that passed from the hot lead into you would return via the neutral lead keeping the gfci happy the two pairs connections gfci outlet are not symmetric one labeled load the other line the incoming power feed must connected the line side the outlet will not protected the load side can used protect all devices downstream from thus whole string outlets can covered single gfci outlet subject where should gfcis used the nec mandates gfcis for 110v 15a 20a single phase outlets bathrooms kitchens within the sink garages unfinished basements crawl spaces outdoors near pool just about anywhere else where you likely encounter water dampness there are exceptions for inaccessible outlets those dedicated appliances occupying fixed space typically refrigerators and freezers and for sump pumps and laundry appliances the cec does not mandate many gfcis particular there requirement protect kitchen outlets most garage basement outlets basement outlets must protected you have dirt floor garage outlets they near the door outside bathrooms and most exterior outlets must have gfcis even you are not required have gfci protection you may want consider installing anyway unless you need gfci breaker see below the cost low the gfci outlets can cost little costs are bit higher canada evaluate your own risk f