there beta version pem being tested the time this writing there are also two programs available the public domain for encrypting mail pgp and ripem both are available ftp each has its own newsgroup alt security pgp and alt security ripem each has its own faq well pgp most commonly used outside the usa since uses the rsa algorithm without license and rsa patent valid only least primarily the usa ripem most commonly used inside the usa since uses the rsaref which freely available within the usa but not available for shipment outside the usa since both programs use secret key algorithm for encrypting the body the message pgp used idea ripem uses des and rsa for encrypting the message key they should able interoperate freely although there have been repeated calls for each understand the other formats and algorithm choices interoperation available this time far know the unix crypt command secure see ree84 there program available called cbw crypt breaker workbench which can used ciphertext only attacks files encrypted with crypt one source for cbw ftpcb how use compression with encryption number people have proposed doing perfect compression followed some simple encryption method xor with repeated key unfortunately you can only compress perfectly you know the exact distribution possible inputs for all practical purposes impossible describe the typical english text beyond coarse characteristics such single letter frequencies you can build more and more sophisticated models your inputs but the enemy has slightly more accurate model able find some redundancy your compressed output note that nearly all practical compression schemes unless they have been designed with cryptography mind produce output that actually starts off with high redundancy for example the output unix compress begins with well known three byte magic number that can serve entering wedge for cryptanalysis this not say that compression before encryption inherently bad idea just has done very very carefully and means removes the need for strong encryption compression after encryption silly there unbreakable cipher yes the one time pad unbreakable see part unfortunately the one time pad requires secure distribution much key material plaintext course cryptosystem need not utterly unbreakable useful rather needs strong enough resist attacks likely enemies for whatever length time the data protects expected remain valid what does random mean cryptography cryptographic applications demand much more out pseudorandom number generator than most applications for source bits cryptographically random must computationally impossible predict what the nth random bit will given complete knowledge the algorithm hardware generating the stream and the sequence 0th through 1st bits for all the lifetime the source software generator also known pseudo random has the function expanding truly random seed longer string apparently random bits this seed must large enough not guessed the opponent ideally should also truly random perhaps generated hardware random number source those who have sparcstation workstations could for example generate random numbers using the audio input device source entropy not connecting anything for example cat dev audio compress foo gives file high entropy not random but with much randomness one can then encrypt that file using part itself key for example convert that seed entropy into pseudo random string when looking for hardware devices provide this entropy important really measure the entropy rather than just assume that because looks complicated human must random for example disk operation completion times sound like they might unpredictable many people but spinning disk much like clock and its output completion times are relatively low entropy what the unicity point unicity distance see sha49 the unicity distance approximation that amount ciphertext such that the sum the real information entropy the corresponding source text and encryption key equals the number ciphertext bits used ciphertexts significantly longer than this can shown probably have unique decipherment this used back claim the validity ciphertext only cryptanalysis ciphertexts significantly shorter than this are likely have multiple equally valid decryptions and therefore gain security from the opponent difficulty choosing the correct one unicity distance like all statistical information theoretic measures does not make deterministic predictions but rather gives probabilistic results namely the minimum amount ciphertext for which likely that there only single intelligible plaintext corresponding the ciphertext when all possible keys are tried for the decryption working cryptologists don normally deal with unicity distance such instead they directly determine the likelihood events interest let the unicity distance cipher characters fewer than ciphertext characters have been intercepted then there not enough information distinguish the real key from set possible keys des has unicity distance characters which less than ciphertext blocks each block corresponds ascii characters this may seem alarmingly low first but the unicity distance gives indication the computational work required find the key after approximately characters have been intercepted fact actual cryptanalysis seldom proceeds along the lines used discussing unicity distance like other measures such key size unicity distance something that guarantees insecurity too small but doesn guarantee security high few practical cryptosystems are absolutely impervious analysis all manner characteristics might serve entering wedges crack some cipher messages however similar information theoretic considerations are occasionally useful for example determine recommended key change interval for particular cryptosystem cryptanalysts also employ variety statistical and information theoretic tests help guide the analysis the most promising directions unfortunately most literature the application information statistics cryptanalysis remains classified even the seminal 1940 work alan turing see koz84 for some insight into the possibilities see kul68 and goo83 what key management and why important one the fundamental axioms cryptography that the enemy full possession the details the general cryptographic system and lacks only the specific key data employed the encryption course one would assume that the cia does not make habit telling mossad about its cryptosystems but mossad probably finds out anyway repeated use finite amount key provides redundancy that can eventually facilitate cryptanalytic progress thus especially modern communication systems where vast amounts information are transferred both parties must have not only sound cryptosystem but also enough key material cover the traffic key management refers the distribution authentication and handling keys publicly accessible example modern key management technology the stu iii secure telephone unit which for classified use employs individual coded crypto ignition keys and central key management center operated nsa there hierarchy that certain ciks are used authorized cryptographic control personnel validate the issuance individual traffic keys and perform installation maintenance functions such the reporting lost ciks this should give inkling the extent the key management problem for public key systems there are several related issues many having with whom you trust can use pseudo random chaotic numbers key stream chaotic equations and fractals produce apparent randomness from relatively compact generators perhaps the simplest example linear congruential sequence one the most popular types random number generators where there obvious dependence between seeds and outputs unfortunately the graph any such sequence will high enough dimension show regular lattice mathematically this lattice corresponds structure which notoriously easy for cryptanalysts exploit more complicated generators have more complicated structure which why