 viral and rickettsial diseases national center for infectious diseases cdc reference cdc cdc surveillance summaries march mmwr 1993 hicnet medical newsletter page volume number april 1993 clinical research news clinical research news for arizona physicians vol april 1993 tucson arizona published monthly the office public affairs the university arizona health sciences center copyright 1993 the university arizona high tech assisted reproductive technologies following the birth the first vitro fertilization embryo transfer ivf baby 1978 host assisted reproductive technologies have been developed that include ivf gamete intrafallopian tube transfer gift embryo cryopreservation freezing and gamete micromanipulation together these technologies are referred the high tech assisted reproductive technology art procedures ovulation induction sperm insemination and surgery for tubal disease and pathology still are the mainstays the therapies available for infertility management however when these fail almost always appropriate proceed with one the art procedures therefore addition comprehensive basic and general infertility service the university arizona center for reproductive endocrinology and infertility there program assisted reproduction that specializes art procedures this program serves tertiary provider for those patients the state arizona whose infertility problems cannot resolved the traditional therapies the following article back describes the art procedures available our center clarifies appropriate applications for each and considers the realistic expectations for their success procedures included are vitro fertilization embryo transfer ivf gamete intrafallopian tube transfer gift cryopreservation human embryos and gamete micromanipulation this article also considers ongoing research our program that directed towards improved success these technologies hicnet medical newsletter page volume number april 1993 future areas research addition ongoing research that directed exclusively toward the management infertile couples are developing the technology assist couples who are risk for producing embryos with serious hereditary disease this technology involves biopsying the preimplantation human embryo and then subjecting the biopsied cells genetic analysis using either dna amplification fluorescent situ hybridization there are recent reports the successful application dna amplification other centers for example for diagnosis the genes for cystic fibrosis and hemophilia hope apply and further focus fluorescent situ hybridization technology for probing the chromosome the identification which will provide scientific basis for counselling patients who exhibit sex linked disorders the considerable clinical application such technology lies the fact that circumvents the need for prenatal diagnosis addition the possibility subsequent termination affected fetuses order avoid the birth affected children catherine racowsky associate professor and director research department obstetrics and gynecology college medicine university arizona tucson arizona applications success rates and advances for the management infertility the following are the art procedures available the university arizona center for reproductive endocrinology and infertility vitro fertilization embryo transfer the core art procedure our assisted reproduction program this procedure involves retrieval unfertilized eggs from the ovary their insemination vitro dish and the culture resultant embryos for days before they are transferred the patient uterus all cultures are maintained incubator under strictly controlled atmospheric and temperature conditions before being processed for use insemination semen samples are evaluated our andrology laboratory using both subjective light microscopy and computer hicnet medical newsletter page volume number april 1993 assisted semen analyses ensure adequate number eggs with which perform ivf indeed gift follicular development typically stimulated with gonadotropins perganol metrodin gonadotropin releasing hormone gnrh factrel lutrepulse and gnrh analogues lupron depo lupron synarel occasionally however ivf accomplished with eggs obtained non stimulated cycles while some programs utilize laparoscopic egg retrieval the operating room with the patient under general anesthesia undertake the less costly approach ultrasound guided retrieval our infertility unit with the patient sedated couples who resort ivf exhibit such pathologies tubal deficiencies ovulatory dysfunction endometriosis and mild forms male factor infertility according the united states ivf registry the overall success rate for ivf nationwide has stabilized about percent per cycle results from our program involving patients who have undergone 173 ivf cycles reflect comparable success rate nevertheless the overall incidence success with this procedure disconcertingly low and emphasizes the need address those physiological factors that limit achievement higher percentage pregnancies well recognized predictors outcome include patient age response exogenous ovarian stimulation quality sperm and number repeated ivf cycle attempts however among these age the single most significant determinant conception therefore critical that such patients are referred assisted reproduction program the earliest opportunity following failure traditional therapies the underlying basis for the negative effect age fertility has not been clearly delineated beyond recognition that the number eggs available for retrieval declines markedly with age fertilization rates significantly decrease eggs retrieved from patients who are over years and provided the appropriate hormonal background present age unrelated uterine competency sustain pregnancy ongoing research our center therefore investigating physiological changes the egg that may impacted age have determined that more than percent eggs that fail fertilize vitro are chromosomally abnormal and that significant proportion these abnormalities are accountable patient age currently the only recourse for such patients use eggs obtained from donor our program has initiated recruitment volunteer egg donors satisfy the needs list recipients interested this form therapy gift this high tech art procedure performed the operating room usually with the use laparoscope and contrast ivf involves introducing sperm and freshly retrieved eggs into the lumen the fallopian tube average eggs tube under these circumstances fertilization occurs vivo and excess eggs are retrieved the remainder undergo ivf with subsequent options for embryo transfer that cycle freezing for transfer subsequent cycle this art procedure applied cases hicnet medical newsletter page volume number april 1993 which there least one patent fallopian tube but the couple has such pathologies ovulatory dysfunction endometriosis male factor infertility and idiopathic infertility the data reported the united states ivf registry for 1985 through 1990 indicate that the overall success rate with gift higher than that obtained with the ivf technique range clinical pregnancies for gift percent and for ivf percent view this fact one might expect more patients treated with gift than ivf however our program have taken into account three basic concerns which while substantially reducing the number gift cycles performed benefit the patient these concerns are the increased costs associated with performing procedure the operating room the risks albeit minimal undergoing general anesthesia and the considerable benefits accrued from obtaining direct information the quality and fertilizability the eggs and the developmental competency resultant embryos the increased success with gift undoubtedly reflects the artificial environment provided the laboratory the ivf procedure between january 1991 and december 1992 have performed total gift cycles with overall success rate percent embryo cryopreservation freezing applied our program when embryos result from residual gift eggs from non transferred ivf embryos this procedure not only provides patients with subsequent opportunity for success much reduced costs but also circumvents the legal and ethical issues relating disposal supernumerary embryos therefore stipulated the american fertility society ethical guidelines for art programs from both practical and ethical standpoint all assisted reproduction programs should have the capability cryopreserving human embrys gamete micromanipulation this art procedure which still very new applied couples who are unaccepting insemination with donor semen but who have severe male factor infertility less than million sperm combination with fewer than perccent motile sperm and less than percent sperm with normal morphology are currently developing the procedure sub zonal insertion szi which entails injecting sperm under the coating around the egg the barrier normally penetrated the sperm through enzymatic digestion available data from szi programs world wide indicate that only percent szi cycles result pregnancy this statistic undoubtedly relates limitations imposed abnormalities inherent the sperm therefore are currently focusing the development improved techniques for the recognition and selection sperm chosen for manipulation such efforts are unquestionably worthwhile view the fact that this technology offers the only realistic opportunity for severe male factor patients establish conception catherine racowsky hicnet medical newsletter page volume number april 1993 associate professor and director research department obstetrics and gynecology college medicine end part internet david stat com fax 602 451 1165 bitnet atw1h asuacad fidonet 114 amateur packet ax25 wb7tpy wb7tpy usa
