disguised message called ciphertext encryption means any procedure convert plaintext into ciphertext decryption means any procedure convert ciphertext into plaintext cryptosystem usually whole collection algorithms the algorithms are labelled the labels are called keys for instance caesar probably used shift encryption for several different values natural say that the key here the people who are supposed able see through the disguise are called recipients other people are enemies opponents interlopers eavesdroppers third parties what references can start with learn cryptology for introduction technical matter the survey articles given part are the best place begin they are general concise authored competent people and well written however these articles are mostly concerned with cryptology has developed the last years and are more abstract and mathematical than historical the codebreakers kahn kah67 encyclopedic its history and technical detail cryptology the mid introductory cryptanalysis can learned from gaines gai44 sinkov sin66 this recommended especially for people who want devise their own encryption algorithms since common mistake try make system before knowing how break one the selection algorithm for the des drew the attention many public researchers problems cryptology consequently several textbooks and books serve texts have appeared the book denning den82 gives good introduction broad range security including encryption algorithms database security access control and formal models security similar comments apply the books price davies pri84 and pfleeger pfl89 the books konheim kon81 and meyer matyas mey82 are quite technical books both konheim and meyer were directly involved the development des and both books give thorough analysis des konheim book quite mathematical with detailed analyses many classical cryptosystems meyer and matyas concentrate modern cryptographic methods especially pertaining key management and the integration security facilities into computer systems and networks the books rueppel rue86 and koblitz kob89 concentrate the application number theory and algebra cryptography how does one about cryptanalysis classical cryptanalysis involves interesting combination analytical reasoning application mathematical tools pattern finding patience determination and luck the best available textbooks the subject are the military cryptanalytics series frie1 clear that proficiency cryptanalysis for the most part gained through the attempted solution given systems such experience considered valuable that some the cryptanalyses performed during wwii the allies are still classified modern public key cryptanalysis may consist factoring integer taking discrete logarithm these are not the traditional fare the cryptanalyst computational number theorists are some the most successful cryptanalysts against public key systems what brute force search and what its cryptographic relevance nutshell and you know and can compute you can find trying every possible that brute force search example say cryptanalyst has found plaintext and corresponding ciphertext but doesn know the key can simply try encrypting the plaintext using each possible key until the ciphertext matches decrypting the ciphertext match the plaintext whichever faster every well designed cryptosystem has such large key space that this brute force search impractical advances technology sometimes change what considered practical for example des which has been use for over years now has about possible keys computation with this many operations was certainly unlikely for most users the mid the situation very different today given the dramatic decrease cost per processor operation massively parallel machines threaten the security des against brute force search some scenarios are described garron and outerbridge gar91 one phase more sophisticated cryptanalysis may involve brute force search some manageably small space possibilities what are some properties satisfied every strong cryptosystem the security strong system resides with the secrecy the key rather than with attempt keep the algorithm itself secret strong cryptosystem has large keyspace mentioned above the unicity distance measure which gives the minimum amount ciphertext that must intercepted uniquely identify the key and for some key the unicity distance much longer than the amount ciphertext you intend encrypt under that key the system probably strong strong cryptosystem will certainly produce ciphertext which appears random all standard statistical tests see for example cae90 strong cryptosystem will resist all known previous attacks system which has never been subjected scrutiny suspect system passes all the tests mentioned above necessarily strong certainly not many weak cryptosystems looked good first however sometimes possible show that cryptosystem strong mathematical proof joe can break this system then can also solve the well known difficult problem factoring integers see part failing that crap shoot cryptosystem theoretically unbreakable then guaranteed analysis proof practice cryptanalytic methods include what known practical cryptanalysis the enemy doesn have just stare your ciphertext until figures out the plaintext for instance might assume cribs stretches probable plaintext the crib correct then might able deduce the key and then decipher the rest the message might exploit isologs the same plaintext enciphered several cryptosystems several keys thus might obtain solutions even when cryptanalytic theory says doesn have chance sometimes cryptosystems malfunction are misused the one time pad for example loses all security used more than once even chosen plaintext attacks where the enemy somehow feeds plaintext into the encryptor until can deduce the key have been employed see kah67 why are many people still using cryptosystems that are relatively easy break some don know any better often amateurs think they can design secure systems and are not aware what expert cryptanalyst could and sometimes there insufficient motivation for anybody invest the work needed crack system
