2 |text from sloan cis uab edu kenneth sloan subject removing distortion from bitmapped drawings organization cis university alabama birmingham lines 135 article 1993apr19 141034 24731 sctc com boebert sctc com earl boebert writes let say you have scanned image line drawing this case boat but could anything the drawing you have set reference points whose true positions are known now you digitize the drawing manually this case using yaron danon excellent digitize program that you use program which converts cursor positions and saves those values when you click the mouse upon digitizing you notice that the reference point values that come out the digitizing process differ small but significant ways from the known true values this understandable because the scanned drawing reproduction the original and there are successive sources distortion such differential expansion and contraction paper errors introduced the printing process scanner errors and what have you the errors are not uniform over the entire drawing global adjustments such stretching contracting uniformly over rotating the whole drawing are not satisfactory the question does any kind soul know algorithm for removing such distortion particular have three sets points reference the known true values distortedreference the same points with known errors distorteddata other points with unknown errors what function reference and distorted could apply distorteddata remove the errors suspect the problem could solved treating the distorted reference points resulting from the projection bumpy surface solving for the surface and then flattening remove the errors the other data points helps have some idea the source the distortion least reasonable model the class distortion below very short description the process which use you have further questions feel free poke via mail assume locally smooth distortion compute the delaunay triangulation your points this defines the set neighbors for each point your data are not naturally convex you may have very long edges the convex hull consider deleting these edges now there are two goals move the distorteddata the reference keep the length measured from the current close possible the digitizedlength measured using the digitized for every point compute displacement based and for example for points for which you know the reference you can move alpha0 reference current this will slowly move the distortedreference towards the reference for all other points examine the current length each edge for each edge compute displacement which would make that edge the correct length where correct the digitizedlength take the vector sum these edge displacements and move the point alpha1 sumofedgedisplacements this will keep the triangulated mesh consistent with your digitized mesh iterate until you are happy for example point moves very much alpha0 and alpha1 need determined experimentation consider how much you believe the reference you absolutely insist the final points exactly matching the references you want balance some error matching the reference against changes length the edges warning there are couple geometric invariants which must observed essentially you can allow the convex hull change and you can allow triangles fold over neighboring triangles both these can handled either special case checks the motion individual points periodically triangulating the points using the current positions but still calculating digitizedlength from the original positions when first did this the triangulation time was prohibitive only did once were motivated try and change code that has been working production mode for years might back and triangulate every iteration you have more compute power than you know what with you might consider having every point interact with every other point but first read linear solutions the body problem there are lots papers the last years siggraph proceedings springs constraints and energy calculations which are relevant the above method described more less detail inproceedings sloan86 author sloan kenneth and david meyers and christine curcio title reconstruction and display the retina booktitle proceedings graphics interface vision interface address vancouver canada pages 385 389 month may year 1986 techreport curcio87b author christine curcio and kenneth sloan and david meyers title computer methods for sampling reconstruction display and analysis retinal whole mounts number institution department computer science university washington address seattle month december year 1987 article curcio89 author christine curcio and kenneth sloan and david meyers title computer methods for sampling reconstruction display and analysis retinal whole mounts journal vision research volume number pages 529 540 year 1989 kenneth sloan computer and information sciences sloan cis uab edu university alabama birmingham 205 934 2213 115a campbell hall uab station 205 934 5473 fax birmingham 35294 1170
