19 |text from ipser solomon technet ipser subject government mandated energy conservation unnecessary and wastful study finds nntp posting host solomon technet lines government mandated energy conservation unnecessary and wastful study finds washington the energy tax and subsidized energy efficiency measures supported president clinton and energy secretary hazel leary are based faulty assumptions new study from the cato institute points out according jerry taylor cato director natural resource studies are not running out sources energy the world now has almost times the proven oil reserves had 1950 and twice the reserves 1970 proven reserves coal and natural gas have increased just dramatically when standards living population densities and industrial structures are controlled for the united states less energy efficient than japan and more energy efficient than many the group seven nations energy independence provides little protection against domestic oil price shocks because the energy economy global moreover since the cost oil represents only about percent gross national product even large increases the price oil would have little impact the overall economy market economies are average times more energy efficient per 000 gnp than are centrally planned economies utilities subsidized energy efficiency measurs known demand side management programs encourage free riders overuse competing resource inputs competitive inequities furthermore dsm programs not reduce demand taylor concludes that government mandated energy conservation imposes unnecessary costs consumers and wastes not conserves energy that subsidizing energy conservation technologies will stymie not advance gains energy conservation and that central control over the lifeblood modern society energy would transfer tremendous power the state the expense the individual energy conservation and efficiency the case against coercion 189 the policy analysis series published the cato institute independent public pol