y 1982 source john dewey the new republic vol nov 1928 268 happy the minority jews which has had christian nation protect and one recalls that the jews took their abode fanatic turkey when they were expelled from europe especially spain saintly christians and they have lived here for centuries least much tranquility and liberty their fellow turkish subjects all being exposed alike the rapacity their common rulers one brought most americans have been the gladstonian and foreign missionary tradition the condition the jews turkey almost mathematical demonstration that religious differences have had influence the tragedy turkey only they were combined with aspirations for political separation which every nation the world would have treated treasonable one readily reaches the conclusion that the jews turkey were fortunate also stated that they armenians traitorously turned turkish cities over the russian invader that they boasted having raised army one hundred and fifty thousand men fight civil war and that they burned least hundred turkish villages and exterminated their population turkey and the holocaust interview with stanford shaw history who recently completed two books the jews the ottoman empire and the turkish republic and turkey and the holocaust turkey role rescuing turkish and european jewry from nazi persecution 1933 shaw chairs the undergraduate interdepartmental degree program near eastern studies and has organized the program for the study ottoman and turkish jewry affiliated with the von grunebaum center for near eastern studies editor how did you come write these two books turkey and european and turkish jews shaw basically ottoman historian but also jewish spent twenty five years studying ottoman history and time went along whenever found materials the ottoman jews collected them but never did anything with them until couple years ago when suddenly realized that 1992 was the 500th anniversary the jews being expelled from spain and coming turkey then the sephardic temple down wilshire avenue invited give series three lectures ottoman jewry these lectures were greatly appreciated and became motivated undertake further research develop book the jews the ottoman empire and the turkish republic this book quite different from the works most jewish historians who tend look the jews any country more from the viewpoint the jews and the jewish community and rely mainly jewish sources view subject ottoman historian and approach the jews the ottoman empire largely from the point view ottoman society using largely ottoman sources after finished this book and sent the press came across additional documents relating turkish jews during world war the completed book had said that turkey had done good deal rescue the jews during world war but did not actually have many details then found batch documents the foreign ministry archive relating actions taken turkish diplomats help the jews before and during the holocaust was too late add this new information the book press decided write second book conducted further research mainly the archives the foreign ministry ankara and the turkish embassy and consulate paris the result was the second book turkey and the holocaust which details how turkey helped rescue jews from the nazis how exactly did they this the story takes place over number years the book presents the material three parts first which deals with the period before the holocaust when the nazis came power germany 1933 they immediately started dismissing jews and anti nazi germans from universities hospitals scientific institutes and the like turkey that moment was just beginning the process reforming its universities and saw these jews who were being fired from their positions germany good source new talent help modernize the turkish universities within three months after the nazis started dismissing these jews turkey arranged take many them they were brought turkey and were given appointments professors the turkish universities heads scientific institutes and medical personnel hospitals about 300 500 major jewish professors came turkey the 1930s ernst reuter german political scientist spent the war years teaching political science turkey after world war was mayor berlin during the berlin airlift fritz neimark major german jewish economist came turkey and helped establish modern school economics istanbul man named reichenbach who was rescued from the nazis turkey and spent the war years turkey eventually came ucla where became professor philosophy other german jewish emigres engaged cultural activities turkey one such was karl ebert who had been leading theatrical producer berlin until was expelled the nazis went turkey where organized the turkish national theater and the turkish national opera company ankara with the help paul hindemuth the first section the book covers this first phase when jews were being persecuted germany and rescued turkey oddly enough the german emigres when they were turkey did not seem think too badly germany they regarded themselves more germans than jews and they did not join the anti nazi activities the local turkish jewish community even found letters from the nazi representatives turkey praising these german jewish refugees for their work promoting the idea german culture even though these people had been persecuted the nazis and rescued the turks they shared the nazis feelings aryan racial superiority over the turks the second part the book deals with the holocaust which began 1940 when the nazis occupied france europe that time and especially france there were about 000 turkish jews they had migrated europe for various reasons from about the turn the century onward most them had settled europe during the turkish war for independence after world war when greece was threatening overrun turkey the greeks had persecuted the jews throughout the nineteenth century and the jews feared what might happen them the greeks took over turkey many jews fled france during the 1920s and 1930s many also abandoned their turkish citizenship and became french citizens suddenly the nazis invaded france 1940 and started introducing all sorts anti jewish laws the turkish jews soon found that was not worth very much french jew but that was worth lot turkish jew how turkey remained neutral through most world war retained its embassies and consulates all the nazi occupied countries until finally entered the war the side the allies the end 1944 during the war therefore turkey was position defend its citizens against anti jewish measures and the actions that turkish diplomats took form the second chapter the book turkish diplomats who were stationed france particular intervened protect jews turkish citizenship from the nazis for those turkish jews who had retained their turkish citizenship there was generally problem they were arrested and sent concentration camp the turkish diplomats would communicate with the commanders the camp and other officials and say effect these people are turkish citizens you can this them and the turkish jews would released their businesses were confiscated the turkish diplomats would protest and the businesses would restored the nazis general wanted keep the friendship turkey they hoped able use turkey gateway for invasion the middle east and they also wanted obtain chromium and manganese from turkey order keep turkish friendship they usually accepted these interventions behalf turkish jews the turkish diplomats sometimes went the concentration camps secure the release turkish jews times they even boarded trains hauling turkish jews auschwitz for extermination and succeeded getting them off the train most the foreign jews were sent concentration camp place called drancy paris and that where most the intercession turkish consuls took place the greater problem came with the turkish jews who had abandoned their turkish citizenship and had become french citizens the consuls couldn declare that these people were turkish citizens because they were not book includes photographs jews lining front the turkish consulate either get passports return turkey get restoration their turkish citizenship this was bureaucratic matter processing the application would take some time the meantime was real emergency because the nazis would arrest jews the streets for almost nothing the nazis would even arrest them they had radios telephones their apartments because radios and telephones were forbidden jews take care these former turkish jews the turkish diplomats invented document called gayri muntazem vatandash irregular fellow citizen the document said effect this person former turkish citizen who has applied for the restoration his turkish citizenship the meantime would appreciate you would treat him were turkish citizen the diplomats wrote the document turkish and put their seals since the nazis could not read turkish the whole they accepted these papers certificates citizenship this means the turkish diplomats were able rescue many jews who had relinquished their turkish citizenship actually the nazis were two minds about the turkish defense jews the one hand the nazi foreign ministry which wanted retain the friendship turkey was favor accepting these interventions the other hand himmler and eichmann wanted all jews exterminated times himmler and eichmann were able prevail and some the turkish jews were sent off auschwitz before the turkish consuls could anything you have statistics how many turkish jews were rescued there were about 000 turkish jews europe before world war about 000 whom were living france most the information this section the book relates the situation france have published the letters that the turkish consuls sent the nazi officials and the letters that came back reply generally the nazis said that the turkish consul would present documents certifying that arrested individuals are turkish citizens and promise send them out france the nazis would release them from the concentration camp the turkish consuls also organized special trains take turkish jews from nazi occupied territory back turkey these trains ran regularly 1943 and 1944 the nazis gave the turkish jews visas they could pass out nazi territory but the trains were often held the nazi influenced governments eastern europe croatia serbia and bulgaria because these governments really didn want the jews escape result the turkish consuls efforts about 000 000 the turkish jews france were saved another 000 were sent off auschwitz where most them died the remaining 000 either escaped across the border into spain fled the area southern france occupied the italians who treated jews much better than the nazis did the end 1943 however italy fell out the war and that was the end for those jews well incidentally the turkish diplomats nazi occupied greece also worked rescue jews that country the second part your book then deals with turkish diplomats acting rescue jews turkish citizenship turkish origin from nazi persecution yes and there aside might add here their interventions behalf turkish jews the turks cited their treaty with germany which stated that turkish citizens german territory would treated the same german citizens turkey that basis the turks maintained that the nazis could not discriminate against turkish citizens who are jews the nazis claimed and the vichy government agreed that they were not discriminating because they were treating all jews equally turkey protested saying you are dividing our citizens according religion but the turkish constitution requires that all citizens treated equally regardless religion therefore you cannot single out turkish jews american consuls paris contrast accepted the nazi argument and told american jews who were being persecuted the nazis that they couldn anything about because the american jews were being treated the same other jews the third part the book takes place turkey which was the principal center during the holocaust for activities aimed the rescue eastern european jews the kwish agency organization established jews palestine help resettle jews palestine set office istanbul 1940 under the leadership chaim barlas other jewish organizations palestine especially the kibbutzes also sent representatives istanbul set headquarters these groups first tried contact jews eastern europe find out what was happening today know about the holocaust but that time people didn know what was going they didn imagine the nazis could the things they were doing and the first step was get information and the turkish government let them use the turkish mails send letters their relatives and friends eastern europe the jewish organizations found out what was happening when they received replies later when the nazis began intercept such letters the jews received assistance also from the vatican nuncio angelo roncali who served the vatican representative istanbul from 1935 1944 and later became pope john xxiii the vatican representative during the war used the facilities the catholic church supplement what the turkish government was doing assist jewish agencies contacting jews eastern europe with the cooperation the turkish government these agencies then sent hard currency food clothing and even railroad and steamship tickets jews czechoslovakia bulgaria romania and hungary they weren able help much poland because then the nazis had wiped out almost all the polish jews whenever possible the rescue agencies arranged for the jews get out eastern europe either train through the called orient express route istanbul boat through the black sea istanbul turkey was not eager for all these refugees remain within its borders during the war because was being blockaded and was suffering terrible shortages food and clothing the government therefore facilitated the movement the non turkish jewish refugees from turkey palestine either the taurus express railroad through the mountains syria and palestine small boats across the eastern mediterranean from southern turkey palestine these efforts were bitterly opposed not only the nazis but also the british who did not want any more jewish immigration palestine because they feared would hurt their relations with the arabs the british constantly pressured the turkish government stop this traffic and send those jews back few cases the turkish government yielding british pressure did send the boats back for example one incident the steamship struma with some 700 jewish refugees from romania was sent back the turkish government result the intervention the british ambassador when that ship was sunk soviet submarine all were lost except one person nevertheless all told the turkish government allowed fewer than 100 000 eastern european jews pass through turkish territory and move palestine during the second world war the turkish authorities also provided these refugees with facilities and money and gave them permission send money and food out the country many these jews who passed through turkey may still living israel yes and their children but let return for moment the first group the turkish jews who came from europe they did not palestine they stayed turkey was the non turkish eastern european jews who passed through turkey route palestine their story very interesting and you have rescued from obscurity many studies have been made the holocaust but most them not focus the eastern european middle eastern jews most the scholarship has centered the western european jews whom million were massacred the nazis study deals with much smaller number people have tried round out the picture and hope book will persuade other scholars undertake further investigations the history eastern jews when comes numbers the german jews were also relatively small number most the millions slain were polish jews the rescue 100 000 eastern european jews may not seem significant compared with the total million who were murdered but meant lot those who were saved about three fourths the book consists documents translations many documents they are included because the story not well known not only are people the west unaware the courageous actions the turkish diplomats even the people turkey did not know the story felt that they would not fully understand this remarkable achievement unless they could see the documents what languages are used the documents most them are turkish french some are hebrew there great deal material hebrew about the organization the boats going palestine the passengers and but did not into those details extensively describe mostly what turkey did most documents are turkish french few documents are english the jewish groups istanbul did not necessarily cooperate with one another rescue jews fact they often fought with one another they took turns trying get the turkish government deport rival groups for example some the kibbutz groups felt that the jewish agency was run western european jews who were interested only helping western european jews finally 1944 president roosevelt sent personal representative ira hirschman who had been executive bloomingdale department store new york city and hirschman managed reconcile their differences the documents related his mission are english also obtained many documents from serge klarsfeld holocaust historian france who mainly worked the french jews his father was killed the nazis gave materials had gathered the german archives the turkish jews didn personally consult the german archives believe that much more can learned from the german archives and hope someone someday will make the effort this new book fits well with your teaching doesn right giving course the history the jews the ottoman empire first gave the course two years ago addition research writing and teaching been actively involved the commemoration the 500th anniversary the coming the jews the ottoman empire among other things helped organize large international conference the subject which was held istanbul 1992 now that your books are finished and the conference has taken place what you plan next working two new books one history the turkish war for independence which took place after world war during the years 1918 1923 the turks warded off the efforts the victorious european powers occupy turkey and end its independence the second book study sultan abdul hamid the last major sultan who ruled from 1876 1909 was important modernizer his own way although also suppressed all sorts political movements stanford shaw received history and british history then shifted near eastern history earning second and princeton doctoral candidate princeton spent two years abroad studying the school oriental and african studies university london the university cairo the american university cairo and the university istanbul taught harvard before coming ucla 1966 his postdoctoral research has been supported the john simon guggenheim foundation the american research institute turkey the social science research council the national endowment for the humanities the fulbright program and isop has received honorary degrees from harvard university and bosporus university bebek istanbul turkey and medals honor for lifetime contributions the fields islamic and turkish studies from the center for research islamic history art and culture istanbul and from the american friends turkey washington addition undertaking many professional service activities and public lectures both the united states and turkey shaw has also produced eight books and one edited volume his history the ottoman empire and modem turkey vols has been published many editions six editions reprints from 1977 1991 and translated into turkish 1983 1991 and french 1984 his book the jews the ottoman empire and the turkish republic macmillan london and new york university press 1992 will published turkish translation the turkish historical society istanbul his turkey and the holocaust turkey role rescuing turkish and european jewry from nazi p