 products mikey1 gif the mikey mikey2 gif schematic picture the mikey tony gif the tony ergonomic keysystem twiddler1 gif front view twiddler2 gif side view vertical gif the vertical keyboard wave gif the iocomm wave keyboard many files are compressed have ending you can uncompress file locally soda will just ask for the file without the extension you unable ftp soda send mail and see what can arrange general info injuries first and foremost importance you experience pain all then you absolutely need see doctor soon you possibly can the difference day two can mean the difference between short recovery and long drawn out ordeal see doctor now your garden variety doctor may not necessarily familiar with this sort injury generally any hospital with occupational therapy clinic will offer specialists these kinds problems don wait though see doctor the remainder this information paraphrased without permission from wonderful report new zealand department labour occupational safety and health service occupational overuse syndrome treatment and rehabilitation practitioner guide first glossary fancy names for how you shouldn have your hands note you likely hear these terms from doctors and keyboard vendors rsi repetitive strain injury general term for many kinds injuries oos occupational overuse syndrome synonym for rsi ctd cumulative trauma disorder another synonym for rsi wruld work related upper limb disorders yet another synonym for rsi cts carpal tunnel syndrome see below hyperextension marked bending joint pronation turning the palm down wrist extension bending the wrist supination turning the palm wrist flexion bending the wrist down pinch grip the grip used for pencil ulnar deviation bending the wrist towards the little finger power grip the grip used for hammer radial deviation bending the wrist toward the thumb abduction moving away from the body overspanning opening the fingers out wide now then problems come two main types local conditions and diffuse conditions local problems are what you expect specific muscles tendons tendon sheaths nerves etc being inflamed otherwise hurt diffuse conditions often mistaken for local problems can involve muscle discomfort pain burning and tingling with identifiable areas tenderness muscles although they not necessarily the problem why does occupational overuse syndrome occur here the theory normally your muscles and tendons get blood through capillaries which pass among the muscle fibers when you tense muscle you restrict the blood flow the time you exerting your full power you completely restricting your blood flow without fresh blood your muscles use stored energy until they run out then they switch anaerobic without oxygen metabolism which generates nasty products like lactic acid which cause pain once one muscle hurts all its neighbors tense perhaps relieve the load this makes sense for your normal sort injury but only makes things worse with repetitive motion more tension means less blood flow and the cycle continues another product the lack blood flow tingling and numbness from your nerves they need blood too anyway when you typing too much you never really giving change for the blood get back where belongs because your muscles never relax enough let the blood through stress poor posture and poor ergonomics only make things worse specific injuries you may have heard note most injuries come two flavors acute and chronic acute injuries are severely painful and noticable chronic conditions have less pronounced symptoms but are every bit real tenosynovitis inflamation the tendon sheath chronic tenosynovitis occurs when the repetitive activity mild intermittent not enough cause acute inflamation but enough exceed the tendon sheath ability lubricate the tendon result the tendon sheath thickens gets inflamed and you got your problem tendonitis inflammation tendon repeated tensing tendon can cause inflamation eventually the fibers the tendon start separating and can even break leaving behind debris which induces more friction more swelling and more pain sub acute tendonitis more common which entails dull ache over the wrist and forearm some tenderness and gets worse with repetitive activity carpal tunnel syndrome the nerves that run through your wrist into your fingers get trapped the inflamed muscles around them symptoms include feeling pins and needles tingling numbness and even loss sensation cts often confused for diffuse condition adverse mechanical tension also known neural tension this where the nerves running down your arm have become contracted and possibly compressed result muscle spasms the shoulders and elsewhere amt can often misdiagnosed associated with one the other oos disorders largely reversible and can treated with physiotherapy brachial plexus stretches and trigger point therapy others for just about every part your body there fancy name for way injure now you should getting idea how oos conditions occur and why just careful many inexperienced doctors misdiagnose problems carpal tunnel syndrome when reality you may have completely different problem always get second opinion before somebody does something drastic you like surgery typing posture ergonomics prevention treatment the most important element both prevention and recovery reduce tension the muscles and tendons this requires learning how relax you under load stress this doubly important tune out the world and breath deep and regular relaxing should become guiding principle your work every three minutes take three second break every three minutes take three second break really every three minutes also helpful work comfortable surroundings calm down and relax you can sleep you really need focus this rest sleep and relaxation are really big deal there are all kinds other treatments course drugs can reduce inflamation and pain custom molded splints can forcefully prevent bad posture surgery can fix some problems exercise can help strengthen your muscles regular stretching can help prevent injury good posture and good ergonomic workspace promote reduced tension ice hot cold contrast baths also reduce swelling only your doctor can say what best for you posture here are some basic guidelines liked the way this was written the new zealand book that lifting almost verbatim from appendix dwallach let your shoulders relax let your elbows swing free keep your wrists straight pull your chin look down don flop your head forward keep the hollow the base your spine try leaning back the chair don slouch slump forward alter your posture from time time every minutes get and bend your spine backward set the seat height first your feet should flat the floor there should undue pressure the underside your thighs near the knees and your thighs should not slope too much now draw yourself your desk and see that its height comfortable work you are short this may impossible the beest remedy raise the seat height and prevent your legs from dangling using footrest now adjust the backrest height that your buttocks fit into the space between the backrest and the seat pan the backrest should support you the hollow your back adjust its tilt give firm support this area you operate keyboard you will able spend more time leaning back experiment with chair with taller backrest available now diverge little from the text good chair makes big difference you don like your chair find better one you really want adjustments for height back angle back height and maybe even seat tilt most arm rests seem get the way although some more expensive chairs have height adjustable arm rests which you can also rotate out the way you should find good store and play with all these chairs pick one that right for you the san francisco bay area highly recommend just chairs the name says all keyboard drawers wrist pads and keyboard replacements there fair amount controvery how get this right for some people wrist pads seem work wonders however with good posture you shouldn resting your wrists anything you would prefer your keyboard right there you drop your arms your side and then lift your hands the elbow you want your keyboard under your hands when your elbows are about degrees course you want avoid pronation wrist extension and ulnar deviation all costs wrist pads may may not help this you should get somebody else come and look how you work how you sit how you type and how you relax often easier for somebody else notice your hunched shoulders deviated hands some argue that the normal flat keyboard antiquated and poorly designed number replacements are available the market today check out the accompanying typing injury faq keyboards for much detail requests for more info clearly the above information incomplete the typing injury archive incomplete there always more information out there you like submit something please send mail and gladly throw you like maintain list products vendors that would wonderful love somebody make list chair desk vendors love somebody make list doctors love somebody edit the above sections looking for places where obviously goofed references completely rewrote the information section here using wonderful guide produced new zealand their occupational safety health service service their department labour special thanks the authors wigley turner blake darby mcinnes and harding semi bibliographic reference occupational overuse syndrome treatment and rehabilitation practitioner guide published the occupational safety and health service department labour wellington new zealand first edition june 1992 isbn 477 3499 price new zealand course thanks richard donkin richardd hoskyns for reviewing this posting dan wallach one the most attractive features connection dwallach berkeley edu machine the array blinking lights the faces office 510 642 9585 its cabinet paris ref manual p48
