14 |text from david stat com david dodell subject hicn610 medical news part reply david stat com david dodell distribution world organization stat gateway service wb7tpy lines 708 cut here university arizona tucson arizona suggested reading tan royston campbell jacobs betts mason edwards 1992 cumulative conception and livebirth rates after vitro fertilization lancet 339 1390 1394 for further information call physicians resource line 800 328 5868 tucson 694 5868 hicnet medical newsletter page volume number april 1993 articles low levels airborne particles linked serious asthma attacks american lung association new study published the american lung association has shown that surprisingly low concentrations airborne particles can send people with asthma rushing emergency rooms for treatment the seattle based study showed that roughly one eight emergency visits for asthma that city was linked exposure particulate air pollution the actual exposure levels recorded the study were far below those deemed unsafe under federal air quality laws people with asthma have inflamed airways and airborne particles tend exacerbate that inflammation said joel schwartz the environmental protection agency who was the lead author the study when people are the threshold having serious asthma attack particles can push them over the edge the seattle study correlated months asthma emergency room visits with daily levels particulate matter with aerodynamic diameter microns less these finer particles are considered hazardous because they are small enough penetrate into the lung cities are considered out compliance with clean air laws the hour average concentration pm10 exceeds 150 micrograms per cubic millimeter air seattle however link between fine particles and asthma was found levels low micrograms the authors concluded that for every microgram increase the four day average pm10 the odds someone with asthma needing emergency treatment increased percent the findings were published the april american review respiratory disease official journal the american thoracic society the lung association medical section the study the latest series recent reports suggest that particulate matter greatly under appreciated health threat 1992 study schwartz and douglas dockery harvard found that particles may causing roughly 000 premature deaths each year the united states other studies have linked particulate matter increased respiratory symptoms and bronchitis children government officials and the media are still very focused ozone says schwartz but more and more research showing that particles are bad actors well one problem setting standards for particulate air pollution that pmio difficult study unlike other regulated pollutants such ozone and carbon monoxide particulate matter complex and varying mixture substances including carbon hydrocarbons dust and hicnet medical newsletter page volume number april 1993 acid aerosols researchers can put people exposure chambers study the effects particulate air pollution says schwartz have way duplicating the typical urban mix particles consequently most what known about particulates has been learned through population based research like the seattle study given that the epa current priority review the ozone and sulfur dioxide standards the agency unlikely reexamine the pm10 standard any time soon until changes are made there appears little people with asthma can protect themselves from airborne particles some areas you can get reports air quality but the reports only cover the pollutant that closest violating its standard and that rarely particulate matter says schwartz however pm10 doesn have near its violation range unhealthy hicnet medical newsletter page volume number april 1993 nih consensus development conference melanoma the national institutes health consensus development conference diagnosis and treatment early melanoma brought together experts dermatology pathology epidemiology public education surveillance techniques and potential new technologies well other health care professionals and the public address the clinical and histological characteristics early melanoma the appropriate diagnosis management and followup patients with early melanoma the role dysplastic nevi and their significance and the role education and screening preventing melanoma morbidity and mortality following days presentations experts and discussion the audience consensus panel weighed the scientific evidence and prepared their consensus statement among their findings the panel recommended that melanoma situ distinct entity effectively treated surgically with centimeter margins thin invasive melanoma less than millimeter thick has the potential for long term survival more than percent patients after surgical excision with centimeter margin elective lymph node dissections and extensive staging evaluations are not recommended early melanoma patients with early melanoma are low risk for relapse but may high risk for development subsequent melanomas and should followed closely some family members patients with melanoma are increased risk for melanoma and should enrolled surveillance programs and education and screening programs have the potential decrease morbidity and mortality from melanoma copy the full text the consensus panel statement available calling the nih office medical applications research 301 496 1143 writing office medical applications research national institutes health federal building room 618 bethesda 20892 hicnet medical newsletter page volume number april 1993 nci designated cancer centers the cancer centers program comprised nci designated cancer centers actively engaged multidisciplinary research efforts reduce cancer incidence morbidity and mortality within the program there are four types cancer centers basic science cancer centers which engage primarily basic cancer research clinical cancer centers which focus clinical research comprehensive cancer centers which emphasize multidisciplinary approach cancer research patient care and community outreach and consortium cancer centers which specialize cancer prevention and control research although some cancer centers existed the late 1960s and the 1970s was the national cancer act 1971 that authorized the establishment new cancer centers well continuing support for existing ones the passage the act also dramatically transformed the centers structure and broadened the scope their mission include all aspects basic clinical and cancer control research over the next two decades the centers program grew progressively 1990 there were comprehensive cancer centers the nation today there are these institutions all which meet specific nci criteria for comprehensive status attain recognition from the nci comprehensive cancer center institution must pass rigorous peer review under guidelines newly established 1990 the eight criteria for comprehensiveness include the requirement that center have strong core basic laboratory research several scientific fields such biology and molecular genetics strong program clinical research and ability transfer research findings into clinical practice moreover five the criteria for comprehensive status significantly beyond that required for attaining cancer center support grant also referred p30 core grant the mechanism choice for supporting the infrastructure cancer center operations these criteria encompass strong participation nci designated high priority clinical trials significant levels cancer prevention and control research and important outreach and educational activities all which are funded variety sources the other types cancer centers also have special characteristics and capabilities for organizing new programs research that can exploit important new findings address timely research questions hicnet medical newsletter page volume number april 1993 the nci designated cancer centers are the basic science type these centers engage almost entirely basic research although some centers engage collaborative research with outside clinical research investigators and cooperative projects with industry generate medical applications from new discoveries the laboratory clinical cancer centers contrast focus both basic research and clinical research within the same institutional framework and frequently incorporate nearby affiliated clinical research institutions into their overall research programs there are such centers today finally consortium cancer centers which there one are uniquely structured and concentrate clinical research and cancer prevention and control research these centers interface with state and local public health departments for the purpose achieving the transfer effective prevention and control techniques from their research findings those institutions responsible for implementing population wide public health programs consortium centers also are heavily engaged collaborations with institutions that conduct clinical trial research and coordinate community hospitals within network cooperating institutions clinical trials together the nci designated cancer centers continue work toward creating new and innovative approaches cancer research and through interdisciplinary efforts effectively move this research from the laboratory into clinical trials and into clinical practice comprehensive cancer centers internet addresses are given where available university alabama birmingham comprehensive cancer center basic health sciences building room 108 1918 university boulevard birmingham alabama 35294 205 934 6612 university arizona cancer center 1501 north campbell avenue tucson arizona 85724 602 626 6372 internet syd azcc arizona edu jonsson comprehensive cancer center university california los angeles 200 medical plaza los angeles california 90027 213 206 0278 hicnet medical newsletter page volume number april 1993 internet rick jccc medsch ucla edu kenneth norris comprehensive cancer center university southern california 14