6 |text from ware cis ohio state edu peter ware subject comp windows intrinsics frequently asked questions faq supersedes faq xt_728840923 oboe cis ohio state edu organization the ohio state university dept computer and info science lines 1609 expires may 1993 gmt reply ware cis ohio state edu nntp posting host oboe cis ohio state edu summary answers about the x11 window system widgets and intrinsics library archive name faq version faq ware exp the toolkit intrinsics monthly posting this article contains the answers some frequently asked questions faq from comp windows about the toolkit intrinsics submit questions preferably with answer send email ware cis ohio state edu many faqs including this one are available the archive site rtfm mit edu the directory pub usenet news answers the name under which faq archived appears the archive name line the top the article this faq archived faq all code fragments are public domain contents glossary software versions related faq why does application core dump when use signals alarms cthreads how use different visual than the default which visual should application use why only shell widgets have visual which visual depth and colormap shells inherit done all the above and still get badmatch error why why doesn widget get destroyed when call xtdestroywidget how exit but still execute the destroycallbacks how resize shell widget why can xtappaddinput handle files what good books and magazines are there what widgets are available what alternatives the intrinsics are there how pass float value xtsetvalues how write resource converter how open multiple displays what changed from where are the resources loaded from what order are callbacks executed how know widget visible how reparent widget xtreparentwidget why use xtmalloc xtfree etc how debug application why don xtaddinput xtaddtimeout and xtaddworkproc work what and how can implement drag and drop glossary the intrinsics implement object oriented interface code allow useful graphical components created included with this are classes that provide the base functionality object rectobj core composite constraint shell overrideshell wmshell etc the terms and intrinsics are used interchangeably however they are used very precisely mean specific library the window system particular does not include the athena motif olit any other widget set without further widgets the intrinsics are not especially useful widget refers user interface abstraction created via the precise use any object that subclass the core class used loosely refer anything that subclass the object class although these are more accurately called windowless widgets gadgets xlib the interface the x11 protocol one layer below the intrinsics typically widget uses relatively few xlib functions because provides most such services although understanding xlib helps with problems software versions the following are the latest versions based software _____________________________________________________________ software version released next expected _____________________________________________________________ x11r4 patch none x11r5 patch athena widgets see x11r5 motif olit xtra x11r4 none xcu x11r5 none fwf _____________________________________________________________ related faq david lewis uunet craft faq maintains the faq posted monthly comp windows and located export contrib faq liam quin lee com posts faq list open look comp windows jan newmarch jan pandonia canberra edu posts faq list motif comp windows motif peter ware ware cis ohio state edu posts faq list for comp windows intrinsics export contrib faq why does application core dump when use signals alarms cthreads brief xlib and most widget sets have mutual exclusion for critical sections any interrupt handler likely leave one the above libraries inconsistent state such all the appropriate flags not yet set dangling pointers the middle list traversal etc note that the ansi standard points out that behavior signal handler undefined the signal handler calls any function other than signal itself this not problem specific xlib and the posix specification mentions other functions which may called safely but may not assumed that these functions are called xlib functions the only safe way deal with signals set flag the interrupt handler this flag later needs checked either work procedure timeout callback incorrect add either these the interrupt handler another note dangerous add work procedure that never finishes this effectively preempts any work procedures previously added and they will never called another option open pipe tell the event loop about the read end using xtappaddinput and then the signal handler can write byte the write end the pipe for each signal however this could deadlock your process the pipe fills why don the intrinsics deal with this problem primarily because supposed portable layer any hardware and operating system that good enough reason don think note the article the journal and the example reilly volume are error how use different visual than the default this requires more complicated answer than should window has three things that are visual specific the visual colormap and border pixmap all widgets have their own colormap and borderpixmap resource only shell widgets have visual resources another questions deals with why shells have visual the default value these resources copyfromparent which does exactly what says the shell widget copyfromparent gets evalulated defaultvisualofscreen and defaultcolormapofscreen when any one the three resources not properly set badmatch error occurs when the window created they are not properly set because each the values depends the visual being used how get this work there are two parts the answer the first you want application start with particular visual and the second you want particular shell within application start with different visual the second actually easier because the basic information you need available the first little harder because you need initialize much the toolkit yourself order determine the needed information some sample code start application using something other than the default visual compile visual visual lxaw lxmu lxt lxext lx11 run visual geometry 300x300 depth visual staticcolor blue yellow you need move the mouse get the particular visuals colormap install include x11 intrinsic include x11 stringdefs include x11 shell typedef struct visual visual int depth optionsrec optionsrec options xtresource resources visual visual xtrvisual sizeof visual xtoffsetof optionsrec visual xtrimmediate null depth depth xtrint sizeof int xtoffsetof optionsrec depth xtrimmediate null xrmoptiondescrec desc visual visual xrmoptionseparg null depth depth xrmoptionseparg null int main argc argv int argc char argv xtappcontext app the application context widget top toplevel widget display dpy display char xargv saved argument vector int xargc saved argument count colormap colormap created colormap xvisualinfo vinfo template for find visual xvisualinfo vinfo_list returned list visuals int count number matchs only arg args cardinal cnt char name test char class test save the command line arguments xargc argc xargv char xtmalloc argc sizeof char bcopy char argv char xargv argc sizeof char the following creates _dummy_ toplevel widget can retrieve the appropriate visual resource cnt top xtappinitialize app class desc xtnumber desc argc argv string null args cnt dpy xtdisplay top cnt xtgetapplicationresources top options resources xtnumber resources args cnt cnt options visual options visual defaultvisualofscreen xtscreen top xtsetarg args cnt xtnvisual options visual cnt now create appropriate colormap could use default colormap based the class the visual could examine some property the rootwindow find the right colormap could all sorts things colormap xcreatecolormap dpy rootwindowofscreen xtscreen top options visual allocnone xtsetarg args cnt xtncolormap colormap cnt now find some information about the visual vinfo visualid xvisualidfromvisual options visual vinfo_list xgetvisualinfo dpy visualidmask vinfo count vinfo_list count xtsetarg args cnt xtndepth vinfo_list depth cnt xfree xpointer vinfo_list xtdestroywidget top now create the real toplevel widget xtsetarg args cnt xtnargv xargv cnt xtsetarg args cnt xtnargc xargc cnt top xtappcreateshell char null class applicationshellwidgetclass dpy args cnt display the application and loop handling all events xtrealizewidget top xtappmainloop app return which visual should application use this point that can argued about but one opinion there way for application know the appropriate visual has specified the user you disagree with this then your application probably falls into the category always using the default visual hardware specific and expects some particular visual such 24bit truecolor with overlayplane extension some such why application runs isolation depending the way server allocates resources may not always want your application run truecolor mode going mess other applications may very upset chooses run greyscale instead psuedocolor just monochrome example low end color sun server there are many different possible visuals monochrome 256 entry colormap static gray static color and truecolor the sgi iris offer all the above plus bit truecolor bit truecolor overlay plane why only shell widgets have visual this strictly convention makes possible for arbitrary widget know that the visual uses can found looking for the shell widget that its ancestor and obtaining the visual that shell widget can have its own visual resource does must have its own realize method use the visual when calls xcreatewindow you should also make this resource that can obtained with xtgetvalues other widgets can find reasonable value probably xtnvisual which visual depth and colormap shells inherit the default value for these resources are set copyfromparent this interpreted the defaultcolormapofscreen defaultdepthofscreen and the default visual the screen the widget has parent applicationshellwidgetclass and the root your widget tree the parent the widget not null then the shell copies colormap and depth from its parent and uses copyfromparent the visual done all the above and still get badmatch error why some resource converters improperly cache references this was especially true x11r3 and earlier versions motif why doesn widget get destroyed when call xtdestroywidget see section the specification eventually does get destroyed just not immediately the intrinsics destroy widget two phase process first and all its children have flag set that indicate being destroyed then put list widgets destroyed this way any pending events further references that widget can cleaned before the memory actually freed the second phase then performed after all callbacks event handlers and actions have completed before checking for the next event this point the list traversed and each widget memory actually free among other things some further caveats trivia the widgets may destroyed the intrinsics determine that they have further references the widgets the list then the phase destruction occurs immediately also nested event loops are used widgets placed the destroy list before entering the inner event loop are not destroyed until returning the outer event loop how exit but still execute the destroycallbacks the problem simple and entirely reasonable approach exiting application used such calling exit directly then widget may not have chance clean any external state such open sockets temporary files allocated resources etc this code for simplicity reasons assumes only single toplevel widget widget toplevelget widget widget find toplevel widget top for top xtparent top top xtparent top empty return top void exitcallback closure call_data widget widget xtpointer closure data the app specified xtpointer call_data widget specific data widget toplevel toplevel toplevelget xtunmapwidget toplevel make disappear quickly xtdestroywidget toplevel exit one can see that the above code exit immediately after destroying the toplevel widget the trouble the phase destruction may never occur this works for most widgets and most applications but will not work for those widgets that have any external state you might think that since works now will always work but remember that part the reason object oriented approach used one can ignorant the implementation details for each widget which means that the widget may change and someday require that some external state cleaned the destroy callbacks one alternative modify exitcallback set global flag and then test for that flag private event loop however private event loops are frowned upon because tends encourage sloppy and difficult maintain practices try the following code instead include x11 intrinsic extern widget toplevelget needfunctionprototypes widget endif extern boolean exitworkproc needfunctionprototypes xtpointer closure endif extern void exitcallback needfunctionprototypes widget xtpointer closure xtpointer call_data endif widget toplevelget widget widget find toplevel widget top for top xtparent top top xtparent top empty return top void exitcallback closure call_data widget widget xtpointer closure data the app specified xtpointer call_data widget specific data widget toplevel toplevel toplevelget xtunmapwidget toplevel make disappear quickly xtdestroywidget toplevel xtappaddworkproc xtwidgettoapplicationcontext exitworkproc xtpointer null boolean exitworkproc closure xtpointer closure exit notreached exitcallback adds work procedure that will get called when the application next idle which happens after all the events are processed and the destroy callbacks are executed how resize shell widget after realized one doesn resize shell widget the proper thing resize the currently managed child the shell widget using xtsetvalues the geometry change then propagated the shell which asks the window manager which may may not allow the request however the shell must have the resource xtnallowshellresize set true otherwise will not even ask the window manager grant the request and the shell will not resize change the position shell use xtsetvalues the shell not the child and within the limits the window manager should granted why can xtappaddinput handle files does however unix semantics for when ready for file does not fit most peoples intuitive model unix terms file descriptor ready for reading whenever the read call would not block ignoring the setting optional flags that indicate not block this works expected for terminals sockets and pipes for file the read will always return but the return indicates eof more data the result the code the intrinsics always calls the input handler because always thinks something about read the culprit the select system call sysv based the poll system call how get around this unix system the best approach use another process check for available input the file use pipe connect the application with this other process and pass the file descriptor from the pipe xtappaddinput suitable program bsd systems tail filename rumored that select some systems not _completely_ reliable particular ibm aix this one where would work for while several thousand times and then stop until some other event woke this seemed the result race condition the kernel ibm claims have fix for this pyramid doesn work all ultrix and possibly others where pipes are implemented sockets wasn completely broken but although the writing side wrote 512 byte blocks the reading side received all broken was being put into the pipe byte time you can waste lot time reading small blocks get raound detecting the situation and having select ignore the pipe for mseconds then had been given the whole block note that all the above descriptions used unix terminology such read file descriptor pipes etc this dependent area and may not identical all systems however the intrinsic designers felt was common enough operation that should included with part the toolkit why they didn also deal with signals this point don know what good books and magazines are there have favorite that the definitive reference perspective offers reasonable introduction but also goes into the full details the intrinsics when started using was already familiar with and the concepts behind newcomers may may not find useful always found accurate and complete which means its 1000 pages asente paul and swick ralph window system toolkit the complete programmer guide and specification digital press 1990 isbn 55558 051 order number e757e and prentice hall isbn 972191 also available through dec direct 800 digital the other book commonly recomend novices young doug the window system applications and programming with motif version prentice hall 1989 isbn 497074 isbn 972167 and course reilly has entire series manuals and reilly ordering 800 998 9938 particular volume reference done manual page style the 3rd edition extensively overhauled and goes far beyond the mit manual pages finding very useful particular the permutted index and references other manual pages help great deal chasing down related information read two periodicals the resource and the the journal these are the only two dealing specifically with the resource published quarterly reilly with one the issues being the mit consortium technical conference proceedings there advertising found informative with pretty good depth for orders call 800 998 9938 email cathyr ora com for editorial matters email adrian ora com table contents are posted math utah edu ftp pub tex bib tex form and ftp net ftp published oreilly xresource ascii form the journal bimonthly trade rag with lots advertising the articles are informative and oriented toward less technical audience read more see what going then with expectation learning great deal but remember represent fairly small percentage people also they have pretty good collection people the advisory board and columnists call