ver became sick and died too soon after birth study depth the present study the scientists used incomplete copy hiv which allowed the animals live longer some the transgenic animals developed scaling wart like tumors their necks and backs other transgenic mice developed thickened crusting skin lesions that covered most their bodies resembling psoriasis humans skin lesions developed their normal non transgenic littermates studies tissue taken from the wart like skin tumors showed that they were type noncancerous tumor called papilloma although the papillomavirus can cause these skin lesions laboratory tests showed sign that virus the animals tissue samples taken from the sick mice throughout the study revealed the presence protein producing molecule made the hiv genetic material hicnet medical newsletter page volume number april 1993 evidence hiv protein production proved that the viral genes were turned active said kopp the scientists found evidence however compromised immunity the mice increase their white blood cell count and signs common infections the fact that hiv genes were active but the animals immune systems were not suppressed confirms that the virus itself was causing the skin lesions kopp said further proof hiv gene involvement came from test which the scientists exposed the transgenic animals ultraviolet light the light increased hiv genetic activity causing papillomas develop formerly healthy skin papilloma formation response increased hiv genetic activity proved the genes were responsible for the skin condition the scientists said lesions appeared normal mice exposed the light the transgenic mice used this study were developed nidr peter dickie who now with the national institute allergy and infectious diseases collaborating the study with kopp were charles wohlenberg drs nickolas dorfman joseph bryant abner notkins and paul klotman all nidr stephen katz the national cancer institute and james rooney formerly with nidr and now with burroughs wellcome 